{
  "id": "convergence",
  "model": "mobile-robots-in-the-plane",
  "name": "Convergence",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Suzuki1999Formation",
    "year": 1999,
    "note": "Suzuki and Yamashita gave algorithms for converging to a point and for reaching a point; Ando et al. treated limited visibility the same year [Ando1999Convergence]."
  },
  "related": [
    {
      "problem": "gathering",
      "relation": "Every gathering algorithm solves convergence, but not conversely: two oblivious robots in SSYNC cannot gather, while \"two oblivious robots can converge to a point with a naive algorithm\" [Izumi2012TwoRobots]. In ASYNC the centre-of-gravity algorithm converges for any $n$ [Cohen2005Gravitational], whereas gathering needs extra assumptions such as multiplicity detection."
    },
    {
      "problem": "near-gathering",
      "relation": "Near-gathering is also a relaxation of gathering: robots must get close (within visibility of each other) without ever colliding [Pagli2015Near], while convergence lets them approach a point only in the limit."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Robots must get arbitrarily close to a common point; they need not ever meet.",
  "statement": "$n$ robots start at arbitrary points. For every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there must be a time after which all robots stay within distance $\\varepsilon$ of each other (equivalently, of a common limit point). Robots never need to coincide.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Think of a flock that must squeeze ever closer together, without ever needing to land on one spot. There are $n$ **robots**, points in the plane; their positions form the **configuration**. Each robot repeats a **Look–Compute–Move cycle**: it takes a **snapshot** of where the other robots are (Look), decides on a destination point (Compute) and moves towards it (Move). They are identical, silent, **oblivious** (no memory between cycles), and each has its own **local coordinate system**; the **scheduler**, an adversary, decides the **activation** of robots, that is, when each performs a cycle. In the basic version the scheduler is ASYNC (each phase may take any finite time, so a robot may act on an outdated snapshot) and every robot sees all others. The robots must converge: for every distance $\\varepsilon > 0$, there must come a time after which all robots stay within $\\varepsilon$ of each other. Unlike gathering, they never have to meet exactly.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Go to the centre of gravity",
        "variant": "async",
        "ref": "Cohen2005Gravitational",
        "idea": "1. Look at the positions of all robots.\n2. Compute their **centre of gravity**: the average of all positions (add up the $x$-coordinates and divide by $n$, and the same for $y$).\n3. Move towards it.",
        "analysis": "The centre of gravity does not depend on the local coordinate system, so robots with different local coordinate systems aim at the same point. In FSYNC (every robot is activated in every **round**) with **rigid moves** (moves that reach their destination), all robots compute the same point and meet in one round. In ASYNC the snapshots are outdated, but each destination is an average of positions that were already inside the **convex hull** of the robots (the smallest convex region containing them), so the convex hull of the robots and their destinations never grows; Cohen and Peleg prove that it actually shrinks to a point [Cohen2005Gravitational]. A later analysis in ASYNC, counting a **round** as a stretch of time in which every robot completes a Look–Compute–Move cycle, shows that with rigid moves $O(n)$ rounds halve the diameter, improving $O(n^2)$, and that this is tight for the centre of gravity [CordLandwehr2011Convergence].",
        "why_it_matters": "It is the simplest convergence rule, and the never-growing convex hull is reused as a building block, for example to split robots into groups [Efrima2009Partition]."
      },
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Short sight: go to the centre of what you see, but keep your neighbours",
        "variant": "limited-ssync",
        "ref": "Ando1999Convergence",
        "idea": "Now each robot sees only the robots within distance $V$ (its **visibility radius**).\n\n1. Compute the **smallest enclosing circle** of the robots you see, and head for its centre.\n2. Do not go farther than a fixed maximum step.\n3. For every robot you see, stay inside the disc of radius $V/2$ around the midpoint between you and it.",
        "analysis": "If two robots that see each other both obey rule 3, both end in the same disc of radius $V/2$, so they are still within $V$ and still see each other (Lemma 2 of [Ando1999Convergence]). Groups therefore never split, the convex hull of each group never grows, and Ando, Oasa, Suzuki and Yamashita prove that each connected group converges to a point in SSYNC with rigid moves (Theorem 1). The argument uses that a whole cycle happens at once; in ASYNC a robot may move on outdated information and break a visibility link. The catalogue records limited-visibility convergence as solved for every bounded amount of asynchrony and open for full ASYNC [Kirkpatrick2024Separating].",
        "why_it_matters": "Its \"stay within $V/2$ of the midpoint\" trick for keeping neighbours is reused by many limited-visibility algorithms, including Go-To-the-Center gathering [Castenow2022Unifying]."
      }
    ]
  },
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "robot",
      "definition": "a computing entity modelled as a point of the plane (a unit disc for fat robots); all robots run the same algorithm",
      "avoid": [
        "agent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "configuration",
      "definition": "the positions of all $n$ robots at one instant, a multiset of points of the plane"
    },
    {
      "term": "Look–Compute–Move cycle",
      "definition": "one activation of a robot: in Look it takes a snapshot, in Compute it determines a destination point from that snapshot alone (and its own light, where lights exist), in Move it travels towards that point",
      "avoid": [
        "Look-Compute-Move cycle"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "snapshot",
      "definition": "the configuration as a robot observes it in Look (with the colours of the lights it sees, where lights exist), expressed in its local coordinate system"
    },
    {
      "term": "oblivious",
      "definition": "a robot keeps no memory from one Look–Compute–Move cycle to the next: Compute uses only the current snapshot"
    },
    {
      "term": "local coordinate system",
      "definition": "the origin, axes, unit of length and handedness in which a robot expresses its snapshot and its destination; robots agree on none of these unless the setting assumes chirality, one common axis or a common compass",
      "avoid": [
        "private coordinate system"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "scheduler",
      "definition": "the adversary that decides when each robot is activated: FSYNC activates every robot in every round, SSYNC a nonempty subset of the robots in each round, the sequential scheduler exactly one robot per round, and ASYNC lets each phase of each cycle take any finite time; every robot is activated infinitely often"
    },
    {
      "term": "activation",
      "definition": "the scheduler's choice to let a robot perform one Look–Compute–Move cycle; a robot that is not activated stays still",
      "avoid": [
        "wake",
        "wakes",
        "woken",
        "awake"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "round",
      "definition": "one time step of FSYNC, SSYNC or the sequential scheduler: each activated robot performs one complete Look–Compute–Move cycle"
    },
    {
      "term": "rigid move",
      "definition": "a move that always reaches the destination computed in Compute"
    },
    {
      "term": "centre of gravity",
      "definition": "the average $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_r p_r$ of the positions $p_r$ of the robots in a snapshot",
      "avoid": [
        "centroid",
        "barycentre"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "convex hull",
      "definition": "the smallest convex region that contains a given set of points (the robots, or the robots and their destinations)",
      "avoid": [
        "region spanned"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "visibility radius",
      "definition": "the distance $V$ within which a robot sees other robots under limited visibility"
    },
    {
      "term": "smallest enclosing circle",
      "definition": "the unique smallest circle that contains a given finite set of points (by default the robots of the configuration), abbreviated SEC; it does not depend on any local coordinate system"
    }
  ],
  "history": "Convergence was introduced alongside gathering as its relaxation [Suzuki1999Formation]. The first questions were **which simple rules converge** under each scheduler (centre of gravity in ASYNC [Cohen2005Gravitational], limited visibility in SSYNC [Ando1999Convergence]); then the **rate** of convergence [CordLandwehr2011Convergence], and how much asynchrony is compatible with limited visibility [Kirkpatrick2024Separating].",
  "measures": [
    {
      "id": "time",
      "name": "Time",
      "description": "rounds (FSYNC, SSYNC) or epochs (ASYNC) until the task is done; \"finite\" when only solvability is known"
    },
    {
      "id": "colours",
      "name": "Colours",
      "description": "colours of the light per robot (0 for oblivious robots)"
    }
  ],
  "ignore_axes": [],
  "axis_order": [
    "body",
    "visibility",
    "opacity",
    "faults",
    "scheduler",
    "memory",
    "frames",
    "movement",
    "multiplicity",
    "algorithm",
    "ids"
  ],
  "variants": [
    {
      "id": "async",
      "title": "ASYNC, unlimited visibility",
      "standard": true,
      "assumptions": {
        "visibility": "unlimited",
        "opacity": "transparent",
        "faults": "none",
        "body": "point"
      },
      "summary": "Moving to the centre of gravity makes oblivious robots converge in ASYNC, for any $n$.",
      "lower_bounds": [],
      "results": [
        {
          "ref": "Cohen2005Gravitational",
          "role": "best",
          "time": "\\text{finite}",
          "colours": "0",
          "note": "Introductory and current best for ASYNC; also tolerates crash faults (the exact bound on $f$ was not checked)."
        }
      ],
      "optimality": {
        "time": {
          "status": "unknown",
          "note": "See the notes for convergence-time results."
        },
        "colours": {
          "status": "tight",
          "note": "No colours."
        }
      },
      "open": [],
      "notes": "Convergence time of the centre-of-gravity algorithm, in epochs (a round of [CordLandwehr2011Convergence] ends once every robot has completed a cycle): with rigid moves, $O(n)$ epochs halve the diameter of the robots' positions and target points, improving the $O(n^2)$ of [Cohen2005Gravitational], and the $\\Omega(n)$ construction of [Cohen2005Gravitational] makes this tight for that algorithm; with non-rigid moves the bound is $O(n + n\\Delta_0/\\delta)$ epochs (Theorem 3 of [CordLandwehr2011Convergence]; their $S$ is $\\delta$ and their $h$ is $\\Delta_0$ here). Their ASYNC model treats Look, Compute and Move as instantaneous operations that other robots' operations may interleave. With axes common to all robots, moving to the centre of the axis-aligned bounding box halves the diameter in $O(1)$ epochs with rigid moves (Theorem 4). Checked against the full text of [CordLandwehr2011Convergence].",
      "status": "verified"
    },
    {
      "id": "limited-ssync",
      "title": "Limited visibility, SSYNC, rigid moves",
      "assumptions": {
        "opacity": "transparent",
        "faults": "none",
        "scheduler": "ssync",
        "body": "point",
        "movement": "rigid"
      },
      "summary": "With limited visibility, semi-synchronous oblivious robots whose moves reach their computed destination converge within each connected component of the visibility graph.",
      "lower_bounds": [],
      "results": [
        {
          "ref": "Ando1999Convergence",
          "role": "best",
          "time": "\\text{finite}",
          "colours": "0",
          "assumes": [
            "each connected component of the initial visibility graph converges to its own point",
            "each move reaches its computed destination, at most a small constant away",
            "$V$ known (the algorithm keeps visible robots within distance $V$)"
          ],
          "note": "Introductory and current best (Theorem 1)."
        }
      ],
      "optimality": {
        "time": {
          "status": "unknown",
          "note": "Time not analysed here."
        },
        "colours": {
          "status": "tight",
          "note": "No colours."
        }
      },
      "open": [],
      "notes": "Checked against the full text of [Ando1999Convergence]. The formal model (assumptions A1–A6) has point robots that do not obstruct each other, phases of \"negligibly small\" duration, so that a robot observes others only when they are stationary (an SSYNC scheduler, although the paper calls the robots asynchronous), every robot active infinitely often, and exact sensing and motion. There is no common coordinate system. A robot moves towards the centre of the smallest enclosing circle of the robots it sees, but no farther than keeps every visible robot within distance $V$ (Lemma 2: mutually visible robots stay mutually visible). Whether the same algorithm works with non-rigid moves is not addressed in the paper.",
      "status": "verified"
    },
    {
      "id": "limited-async",
      "title": "Limited visibility, asynchronous",
      "assumptions": {
        "opacity": "transparent",
        "faults": "none",
        "body": "point"
      },
      "summary": "With limited visibility, convergence is solved for every bounded asynchrony (k-ASYNC) but open for full ASYNC.",
      "lower_bounds": [],
      "results": [
        {
          "ref": "Katreniak2011Convergence",
          "role": "introductory",
          "time": "\\text{finite}",
          "colours": "0",
          "assumes": [
            "1-ASYNC"
          ],
          "note": "Theorem 7, in 1-bounded asynchrony: while a robot goes from its Look to the end of its move, no other robot makes more than one Look; non-rigid moves, no multiplicity detection. The same paper solves unlimited-visibility ASYNC convergence without multiplicity detection (move halfway towards the furthest robot), and it states that the appendix of its full version (not read) proves that the algorithm of [Ando1999Convergence] fails in 1-bounded asynchrony."
        },
        {
          "ref": "Kirkpatrick2024Separating",
          "role": "best",
          "time": "\\text{finite}",
          "colours": "0",
          "assumes": [
            "k-ASYNC for any fixed bound on the asynchrony"
          ],
          "note": "Also shows that cohesive convergence is impossible in ASYNC for hull-diminishing or coherent algorithms when perception has errors."
        }
      ],
      "optimality": {
        "time": {
          "status": "unknown",
          "note": "Full ASYNC is open."
        },
        "colours": {
          "status": "tight",
          "note": "No colours."
        }
      },
